<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="papaverine biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7363" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Papaverine is a benzylisoquinoline alkaloid that was first discovered in 1848. It is usually found a minor component in most of the opium poppy varieties. Papaverine has several medicinal activities such as antispasmodic |CITS: [6529614]| and vasodilatory. It has been used in clinic treatment of cerebral vasospasm |CITS: [15990041]|. Its biosynthetic route was initially proposed almost a century ago however is still controversial and under debate. Mainly there are two proposed routes, the N-demethylation pathway starting from (S)-reticuline as supported by a recent radio tracer study |CITS: [20494383]|, and the (S)-coclaurine-derived pathway, supported by comparative transcriptome evidence |CITS: [23738019]|. Only three enzymes of the proposed routes have been identified and characterized, namely 7OMT in the N-demethylation pathway, N7OMT in the (S)-coclaurine-derived pathway, and an oxidase catalyzes the last step shared by both routes.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="hawkins" />
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
